NXR-026|Boot device registry|Devices|The device registry shall accept unique boot-defined names and static driver objects before sealing at scheduler start.|Drivers currently require single-owner integration without a common registry.|Duplicate names, null operation tables, capacity overflow and post-start registration fail unchanged; lookup returns the exact registered instance or not-found.|Registry capacity, duplicate, seal and lookup tests.|NXR-001|Optional devices; positive maximum device count; names at most 31 bytes.|Fixed registry entries and copied bounded names; zero runtime allocation.|docs/drivers.rst,include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp|Driver guidance reserves objects statically but no device registry API exists.
NXR-027|Unified device operations|Devices|Devices shall expose bounded read, write and typed control operations returning status and transferred byte count, with explicit nonblocking or absolute-deadline modes.|A consistent contract avoids per-driver ambiguity and indefinite calls.|Unsupported operations return not-supported; zero-length operations transfer zero; short transfers report exact progress; expired deadlines never wait; control rejects unknown commands and incorrect payload sizes unchanged.|Mock-driver short transfer, unsupported, deadline and control validation tests.|NXR-009,NXR-026|Fixed operation table per device; finite control command set.|Caller buffers only; request state bounded by configured concurrent operations.|docs/drivers.rst,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,src/ethernet.cpp|Ethernet has explicit busy/error callback contracts but no general read/write/control interface exists.
NXR-028|Bounded descriptor handles|Descriptors|Opening a device or file shall acquire a slot from a boot-reserved descriptor table and return an index-plus-generation handle.|Unix-style handles need finite capacity and stale-reference protection.|Exactly configured slot count opens succeed; excess returns resource-exhausted; close/reopen invalidates old handles; generations never wrap into reuse because exhausted-generation slots retire until reboot.|Exhaustion, stale handle, invalid index and near-generation-limit tests.|NXR-026,NXR-027|Optional descriptors; configured slot count; 32-bit generation.|Fixed descriptor records; no heap; retired slots remain counted unavailable.|include/rtos.hpp,docs/memory.rst|Current API exposes Thread pointers only; no descriptor table or generation management exists.
NXR-029|Descriptor close lifecycle|Descriptors|Close shall mark a valid descriptor closing, reject new operations, wait only until its supplied deadline for in-flight operations and release the slot exactly once after quiescence.|Freeing slots during active driver access would corrupt ownership.|Double close returns invalid-handle; timed close returns timeout while retaining closing state; resumed close completes after drain; driver-close failure preserves a diagnosable unavailable slot.|Close versus read/write races, timeout retry and injected close-failure tests.|NXR-028,NXR-020|Finite per-slot active-operation counter; close uses absolute deadline.|Fixed closing state and active count per descriptor; no detached cleanup allocation.|docs/memory.rst,docs/drivers.rst,include/rtos.hpp|Existing ownership guidance does not define descriptor teardown or concurrent close.
NXR-030|Bounded pathname resolution|VFS|The VFS shall resolve absolute bounded paths with collapsed repeated separators and dot components, rejecting parent traversal above root and all symbolic links.|A small namespace must avoid recursive and unbounded resolution.|Overlong paths/components fail before provider calls; /a/../b resolves /b; /../b fails invalid-path; relative paths and embedded null payloads are rejected; no symlink following occurs.|Path normalization model and malformed/boundary input tests.|NXR-026|Optional VFS; positive maximum path bytes, component bytes and component count.|Fixed normalization workspace per configured concurrent resolver; traversal bounded by component count.|docs/configuration.rst,docs/memory.rst,include/rtos.hpp|No pathname API or VFS configuration exists; static memory rules require bounded resolver workspace.
NXR-031|Static device filesystem|VFS|A devfs provider shall expose sealed device-registry entries under /dev and route opens through descriptor ownership rules.|The accepted Unix-style device namespace should reuse registry identity.|Every registered exported device appears once; unknown names return not-found; directory iteration is bounded and stable; create, unlink and rename return read-only.|Registry-to-devfs mapping, stable enumeration and forbidden mutation tests.|NXR-026,NXR-028,NXR-030|Optional devfs; boot export flag per device.|No duplicated driver objects; at most one fixed directory record per exported device.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst|Drivers are listed in documentation but /dev has no implementation.
NXR-032|Read-only ROM filesystem|VFS|A ROM filesystem shall expose a boot-linked immutable file table with bounded offset reads and no executable loading.|Static assets and help text need file access without writable storage complexity.|Reads at EOF return zero; short tail reads are exact; overflowing offsets and invalid table ranges fail; all writes and namespace mutations return read-only; content bytes match linked assets.|ROM image table validation, offset boundaries and content checksum tests.|NXR-028,NXR-030|Optional ROMFS; fixed file count and linked asset byte budget.|Immutable table and linked bytes; per-open offset stored in descriptor slot.|docs/memory.rst,ports/riscue/link.ld,CMakeLists.txt|The linker builds one static firmware image; no filesystem or executable loader exists.
NXR-033|Bounded RAM filesystem|VFS|Optional RAMFS shall use boot-reserved inode and data-block pools, permit only regular files and directories and return explicit exhaustion without growing storage.|Writable temporary files must preserve the no-heap contract.|Inode/block exhaustion leaves existing contents intact; writes may return documented exact short counts; unlink of open files reclaims blocks only after last close; truncate zeroes newly visible bytes; reboot discards contents.|Pool exhaustion, short write, open-unlink, truncate and repeated create/delete conservation tests.|NXR-028,NXR-029,NXR-030|Optional RAMFS; fixed inode count, block size, block count and directory-entry capacity.|Exactly configured inode, directory and block pools; ownership counters conserve every slot.|docs/memory.rst,src/memory.cpp|Memory helpers exist but no writable filesystem or fixed block ownership manager exists.
NXR-034|Boot mount table|VFS|Mounts shall be registered only at boot with unique absolute normalized prefixes, then remain immutable; resolution shall select the longest component-boundary prefix.|Immutable mounting keeps lookup and provider lifetime bounded.|Duplicate prefixes and mount-table overflow fail; /rom does not match /romance; nested mount chooses longest match; runtime mount/unmount returns sealed.|Prefix boundary, nested mount, duplicate, capacity and boot seal tests.|NXR-030,NXR-031,NXR-032|Fixed mount count; optional devfs, ROMFS and RAMFS providers.|Fixed mount table and bounded copied prefixes; lookup scans at most configured mounts.|docs/configuration.rst,docs/memory.rst|There are no current mount options; configuration guidance requires lifecycle and budgets before exposure.
NXR-035|Static byte pipes|IPC|A pipe shall use a boot-reserved byte ring and bounded endpoint slots, provide partial FIFO transfers and resolve endpoint closure without dynamic allocation.|Pipes provide useful Unix-style composition without processes.|Empty pipe with writers returns would-block or waits to deadline; empty pipe without writers returns EOF; write without readers returns broken-pipe; close wakes affected waiters; byte order survives ring wrap.|Ring wrap, full/empty, deadline, endpoint-close races and endpoint exhaustion tests.|NXR-020,NXR-028,NXR-029|Optional pipes; fixed pipe count, bytes per pipe and reader/writer endpoint limits.|Fixed rings and endpoint records; total queued bytes never exceeds configured storage.|docs/memory.rst,include/rtos.hpp,src/memory.cpp|No pipe API exists; static byte storage and exact endpoint teardown must be added.
NXR-036|Built-in command registry|Shell|The shell shall dispatch only a sealed boot-defined table of built-in C++ command functions with unique bounded names.|A Unix-like console need not load programs or create processes.|Duplicate/overlong names fail at boot; unknown commands return command-not-found; dispatch invokes only registered pointers; registry overflow fails unchanged.|Registry validation, unknown command and dispatch identity tests.|NXR-026|Optional shell; fixed command count and maximum command-name bytes.|Fixed command table; all command code linked into firmware.|include/rtos.hpp,docs/configuration.rst,CMakeLists.txt|Firmware is statically linked and no shell registry or process loader exists.
NXR-037|Bounded shell parser|Shell|The shell shall parse bounded lines with whitespace separation, single/double quoted arguments and backslash escapes, rejecting unsupported expansion, redirection and pipeline syntax initially.|Explicit small grammar avoids accidental unbounded Unix-shell expectations.|Overlong line, too many arguments, unmatched quotes, trailing escape and unsupported syntax return parse errors without command execution; quoted spaces and empty arguments preserve exact bytes.|Grammar corpus, boundary tests and deterministic fuzzing against reference parser.|NXR-036|Fixed maximum line bytes and argument count; no environment expansion or command substitution.|One fixed line buffer and argument-index array per shell session.|docs/memory.rst,include/rtos.hpp|No parser exists; caller-owned storage policy rules out dynamically growing argument vectors.
NXR-038|Static shell sessions|Shell|Each enabled shell session shall own boot-reserved input/output buffers and a boot-created worker, with bounded editing and output backpressure.|Console convenience must not consume unbounded kernel memory or stall unrelated threads.|Session capacity is fixed; line overflow discards until newline and reports one error; output waits only to deadline and reports truncation; disconnected sessions release no borrowed buffer prematurely.|Maximum sessions, oversized line recovery, slow output and disconnect tests.|NXR-027,NXR-036,NXR-037|Fixed session count, worker priority/stack and input/output ring bytes.|Exactly configured session buffers and stacks; no runtime worker creation.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst,include/rtos.hpp|Current kernel seals thread creation at start, so interactive sessions require boot-created workers.
NXR-039|Jobs on boot workers|Shell|Background commands shall run only on a fixed boot-created worker pool with generation-tagged job slots and cooperative cancellation.|Provide limited jobs without fork, executable loading or runtime stacks.|Pool exhaustion returns busy; completed slots invalidate stale job IDs; cancellation requests never forcibly unwind stacks; noncooperating jobs remain occupied and are reported stuck after configured deadline.|Pool exhaustion, stale IDs, completion/cancel races and noncooperating-job tests.|NXR-001,NXR-020,NXR-036,NXR-038|Optional jobs; fixed worker/job count, stacks and cancellation grace ticks.|One permanent stack/control block per worker plus fixed job records and bounded copied arguments.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,docs/api.rst|create is boot-only and exit does not unwind active objects; background work cannot safely imply dynamic tasks or forced cancellation.
NXR-040|Bounded diagnostic commands|Shell|Built-in ps, top, uptime and devices commands shall consume coherent bounded snapshots and print explicit truncation indicators without holding kernel locks during formatting or output.|Existing thread metrics should become usable from the console without long masked I/O.|ps reports name/state/base/effective priority; top handles zero elapsed time and disabled metrics explicitly; rows never exceed caller capacity; slow console output occurs after snapshot lock release.|Snapshot-to-command golden outputs, truncation, disabled metrics and slow-console instrumentation.|NXR-006,NXR-026,NXR-036,NXR-038|Optional diagnostics; fixed maximum rows and output bytes per command invocation.|Fixed snapshot rows and formatting buffer per session; one snapshot per refresh.|include/rtos.hpp,src/kernel.cpp,docs/metrics.rst|snapshot already returns bounded rows and priority/runtime fields; no shell commands or base/effective split exists.
NXR-041|UART bounded transport|Drivers|The UART driver shall use fixed RX/TX rings, expose nonblocking/deadline transfers and defer interrupt processing through approved bounded paths.|UART is the documented first board addition and console prerequisite.|RX overflow drops newest byte and increments a saturating counter; TX full returns short count or waits only to deadline; framing/parity/overrun errors are counted; loopback preserves accepted byte order.|Hardware/model loopback, ring saturation, error injection and deadline tests.|NXR-014,NXR-015,NXR-027|Optional UART instances; fixed ring bytes, baud and framing selected at boot.|Fixed per-instance rings/control state; ISR work capped at configured bytes per interrupt.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/configuration.rst|Driver inventory proposes UART rings and loopback tests; no UART implementation exists.
NXR-042|Console routing and fault output|Console|Console input/output shall route to one boot-selected device; panic output shall use a bounded polling path with a fixed byte/time limit and no descriptor locks.|Diagnostics must remain bounded when scheduler or locks fail.|Normal output follows device backpressure; panic writes at most configured bytes and stops at deadline or unavailable device; recursive panic terminates immediately without lock acquisition.|Slow/stuck UART, recursive panic and scheduler-disabled output tests.|NXR-027,NXR-041|One boot console; optional panic output with positive byte and tick/cycle limits.|Fixed panic buffer and recursion flag; no worker or memory acquisition in panic.|docs/drivers.rst,src/kernel.cpp|stop currently disables interrupts and loops; no console routing or bounded emergency output exists.
NXR-043|GPIO ownership and safe state|Drivers|GPIO shall enforce boot-declared pin ownership, validated direction/functions and board-defined safe output values on failed initialization or explicit shutdown.|Uncoordinated register access can affect unrelated pins and unsafe outputs.|Conflicting claims and unsupported pin/function fail before hardware changes; writes affect only owned pins; output values are established before enabling drive; failure restores declared safe state.|Mock-register ownership/mask tests and board safe-state measurements.|NXR-026,NXR-027|Optional fixed pin table and owner map; board safe values required for outputs.|One fixed record per declared pin; no runtime pin claims.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst|Inventory requests pin ownership and atomic register semantics but no GPIO contract is implemented.
NXR-044|SPI bounded transactions|Drivers|SPI shall accept transactions from fixed boot request slots, serialize each bus and hold chip select only for one bounded transaction with an absolute completion deadline.|Bus sharing and chip-select lifetime require explicit ownership.|Validate mode/rate/length before assertion; preserve segment order; queue full returns busy; timeout deasserts chip select and returns transferred count; subsequent requests recover or receive unavailable.|Mode validation, contention, full queue, timeout and chip-select waveform tests.|NXR-009,NXR-016,NXR-026,NXR-027|Optional buses; fixed request count, maximum segments/bytes and supported rates/modes.|Fixed request/control slots and configured DMA buffers; no retained stack buffer beyond synchronous call.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst|SPI inventory lists slots, chip-select ownership and timeouts without an implemented driver.
NXR-045|I2C error and recovery bounds|Drivers|I2C shall serialize bounded address transactions, report NACK/arbitration/timeout distinctly and attempt at most nine recovery clocks followed by STOP before marking a stuck bus unavailable.|A stuck peripheral must not block a worker indefinitely.|Invalid addresses/lengths fail before START; repeated-start sequences preserve atomic ownership; NACK returns exact progress; recovery is attempted once per failed transaction and never exceeds deadline; unavailable buses reject requests until explicit bounded reinitialize.|NACK, arbitration loss, clock stretch, stuck SDA and recovery waveform tests.|NXR-009,NXR-016,NXR-026,NXR-027|Optional buses; fixed request count/bytes; standard 7-bit addressing initially.|Fixed transaction slots and one bus recovery state per instance.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/configuration.rst|I2C inventory identifies NACK/stuck-bus concerns but defines no recovery bound or implementation.
NXR-046|Progress-based watchdog supervision|Drivers|A boot-created supervisor shall feed the hardware watchdog only when every enabled critical thread advances its registered progress token within its configured monotonic interval.|Timer interrupts alone do not demonstrate application progress.|A stuck critical worker prevents feeding by its deadline; token wrap is handled; noncritical workers do not inhibit feeding; first missed participant is recorded before reset; watchdog setup is validated against supervisor period.|Stalled worker, interrupt-only activity, token wrap, false-positive margin and actual reset tests.|NXR-001,NXR-009,NXR-012,NXR-026|Optional supervisor; fixed critical participant list and positive per-participant deadlines.|Fixed token/deadline records plus one boot supervisor stack; one scan per supervision period.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/configuration.rst,include/rtos.hpp|Documentation recommends progress-based feeding and reset records; no supervisor or watchdog driver exists.
NXR-047|Ordered driver boot failure|Boot|Drivers shall initialize from a fixed dependency-ordered boot table; failure shall disable transitive dependents and unwind successfully initialized affected devices in reverse order to safe state.|Partial initialization must not leave workers using unavailable hardware.|Cycle/unknown dependency fails before hardware access; init order follows dependencies; injected failure prevents dependent initialization; reverse shutdown is called once; critical failure prevents scheduler start while optional failure is exposed diagnostically.|Dependency graph validation and failure injection at every boot-table position.|NXR-026,NXR-043|Fixed init entry count; critical/optional classification and bounded init/shutdown deadlines.|Fixed table and visited/state arrays; traversal bounded by configured entries/edges.|docs/configuration.rst,docs/drivers.rst,src/kernel.cpp|Boot/recovery backlog proposes ordered init and partial-start tests but start has no driver dependency orchestration.
NXR-048|Device request cancellation|Devices|Timed device operations shall transfer buffer ownership back only after hardware/DMA access has stopped; unsuccessful abort shall quarantine the fixed request slot and return a distinct fault result.|Timeout alone cannot make asynchronous buffer reuse safe.|Successful timeout/abort proves device ownership revoked before return; failed abort retains driver-owned fixed buffers and quarantines slot; late completions cannot target reused generation; repeated abort is idempotent.|Delayed DMA completion, abort success/failure, stale completion and slot quarantine tests.|NXR-027,NXR-029|Fixed abort deadline and request-generation width; asynchronous drivers use driver-owned buffers.|Fixed request slots and staging buffers; quarantined capacity never silently reused.|docs/memory.rst,docs/drivers.rst,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp|Ethernet explicitly forbids retaining caller TX pointers; broader timed/DMA operation cancellation is unspecified.
NXR-049|Bounded readiness polling|Devices|A readiness API shall inspect a bounded caller-supplied descriptor set for read/write/error readiness and wait atomically to a deadline without consuming data.|Shell and worker loops need multiplexing without polling every device indefinitely.|Oversized sets fail before driver calls; already-ready returns immediately; readiness transition between registration and sleep is not lost; closed/stale handles report per-entry error; timeout returns zero ready entries.|Ready-before-wait, transition race, close race, invalid handle and maximum-set tests.|NXR-020,NXR-027,NXR-028,NXR-029|Optional readiness; configured maximum descriptors per wait.|One boot-reserved readiness record per waiting thread and bounded descriptor indices.|include/rtos.hpp,docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst|Current yield/sleep APIs provide no multi-device readiness mechanism or atomic readiness wait.
NXR-050|Coherent device diagnostics|Devices|Each driver shall expose a bounded typed health snapshot containing availability, fault reason, capacity/occupancy, overflow/error counts and reset generation without clearing counters on read.|Operators need to distinguish absent hardware, resource exhaustion and driver faults.|Snapshots are internally coherent; counters saturate at maximum rather than wrap; repeated reads have no reset side effect; disabled features return not-supported; fixed output truncation reports required/written rows.|Concurrent update/snapshot, saturation, disabled driver and bounded enumeration tests.|NXR-026,NXR-027,NXR-047|Optional device metrics with fixed per-driver schema and maximum snapshot rows.|Fixed per-device counters/state and caller-owned snapshot rows; no formatting under driver locks.|docs/drivers.rst,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,src/ethernet.cpp,include/rtos.hpp|Ethernet exposes unsynchronized owner-thread counters while thread snapshot reports required/written rows; no unified device health snapshot exists.
